<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  behavior_tree.xml (problem3)

  A Bug0-style tree: go straight to the goal while the direct path is
  clear; if an obstacle sits on that path, follow its boundary until
  line of sight to the goal is regained, then resume straight.

  <Fallback>
    <Sequence>                     : Branch 1: path is clear
      <Inverter><ObstacleOnPath/></Inverter>
      <PlanStraight/>
      <MoveTo triggers="collision;battery;obstacle_on_path(T)"/>
    </Sequence>
    <Sequence>                     : Branch 2: obstacle blocks the path
      <ObstacleOnPath/>
      <FollowBoarder/>
      <MoveTo triggers="collision;battery;line_of_sight_clear(...)"/>
    </Sequence>
  </Fallback>

  Both ObstacleOnPath conditions are written EXPLICITLY near the root,
  one per branch (plain, and inverted), so the tree's own shape reads
  as the reactive condition it represents: "which branch applies"
  reacts to whether an obstacle sits on the direct path: even though
  this project's action theory is a ONE-SHOT Golog/ProbLog plan, not a
  live re-ticked BT.cpp tree. The actual REACTIVE enforcement (an
  obstacle appearing ON the path DURING Branch 1's own walk, or line of
  sight clearing DURING Branch 2's own boundary walk) is realized the
  only way this theory currently can: as a TRIGGER on the enclosing
  MoveTo leaf (obstacle_on_path(T) / line_of_sight_clear(...) in each
  branch's own triggers="..." above): the explicit Condition near the
  root and the Trigger down at the leaf are the SAME underlying
  condition, playing two different roles: the root-level Condition
  documents "this branch only applies when..." for whoever reads the
  tree, while the leaf-level Trigger is what actually halts a walk in
  Poss(haltMoveto(...)) the moment that condition comes true mid-walk.
  When Branch 1's own MoveTo halts via its obstacle_on_path(T) trigger
  (Status=false, since the walk was cut short before its own nominal
  endpoint), Branch 1's Sequence FAILS as a whole: fallback_node's own
  documented "try the next branch from wherever the failed one left us"
  semantics (see basic_action_theory.pl's own note above its
  fallback_node clause) is what then hands control to Branch 2 WITHOUT
  needing any explicit halt-reason check (no HaltedWith node): the
  robot is exactly where the obstacle_on_path trigger caught it,
  current_walk/5-visible, ready for Branch 2's own ObstacleOnPath
  precondition and FollowBoarder planner to pick up from there.

  KNOWN GAPS, NOT YET SUPPORTED by module/translators/bt_to_prolog.py
  (this file is authored ahead of that work, per plan: it will not
  translate/validate cleanly until the translator catches up):
    1. <Inverter> (BT.cpp's built-in negation decorator) has no
       translation branch yet: _CONTROL_FLOW only knows Sequence/
       Fallback.
    2. ObstacleOnPath used as a plain Condition LEAF (not inside a
       Triggers list) translates today into a hard cond(obstacle_on_path
       (Threshold)) precondition check via holds/2, which needs an
       ALREADY-IN-PROGRESS walk (current_walk/5) to mean anything :
       it always fails at s0/before any walk. As used HERE (in a
       Fallback whose branches themselves start a fresh walk), the
       translator needs to recognize this specific "guard for a
       Sequence starting a new walk" shape and treat it as documentation
       rather than a literal precondition: or the theory needs an
       equivalent that also makes sense against a HYPOTHETICAL
       not-yet-planned straight path. Exactly the follow-up work already
       planned.
    3. obstacle_id below is a LITERAL placeholder (obs3: the obstacle
       this map's own straight start->goal line actually crosses,
       picked so the tree is at least geometrically meaningful once it
       does translate), not dynamically bound from Branch 1's own
       obstacle_on_path(T,ObstacleId) trigger outcome the way a real
       Bug0 needs (whichever obstacle actually blocked THIS run, not
       always the same one): binding it dynamically is more translator
       work, not yet built.

  goal="11.675;11.525" matches this same directory's own goal_formula.pl:
  see problem0/behavior_tree.xml's own header for the shared
  goal/config-consistency rationale.
-->
<root BTCPP_format="4" main_tree_to_execute="MainTree">
  <BehaviorTree ID="MainTree">
    <Fallback name="Bug0">
      <Sequence name="PathClear">
        <Inverter>
          <ObstacleOnPath threshold="0.6"/>
        </Inverter>
        <PlanStraight goal="11.675;11.525" control_points="{cp}"/>
        <MoveTo control_points="{cp}" triggers="collision;battery;obstacle_on_path(0.6)"/>
      </Sequence>
      <Sequence name="FollowObstacleBoundary">
        <ObstacleOnPath threshold="0.6"/>
        <FollowBoarder obstacle_id="obs3" offset="0.6" control_points="{cp2}"/>
        <MoveTo control_points="{cp2}" triggers="collision;battery;line_of_sight_clear(obs3,11.675,11.525)"/>
      </Sequence>
    </Fallback>
  </BehaviorTree>

  <TreeNodesModel>
    <Action ID="MoveTo">
      <input_port name="control_points" type="std::vector&lt;Point&gt;"/>
      <input_port name="triggers" type="std::vector&lt;std::string&gt;"/>
      <output_port name="reason" type="std::string"/>
      <output_port name="status" type="bool"/>
    </Action>
    <Action ID="PlanStraight">
      <input_port name="goal" type="Point"/>
      <output_port name="control_points" type="std::vector&lt;Point&gt;"/>
      <output_port name="reason" type="std::string"/>
      <output_port name="status" type="bool"/>
    </Action>
    <Action ID="FollowBoarder">
      <input_port name="obstacle_id" type="std::string"/>
      <input_port name="offset" type="double"/>
      <output_port name="control_points" type="std::vector&lt;Point&gt;"/>
      <output_port name="reason" type="std::string"/>
      <output_port name="status" type="bool"/>
    </Action>
    <Condition ID="ObstacleOnPath">
      <input_port name="threshold" type="double"/>
    </Condition>
    <Condition ID="AtGoal">
      <input_port name="goal" type="Point"/>
      <input_port name="tolerance" type="double"/>
    </Condition>
    <Condition ID="HaltedWith">
      <input_port name="reason" type="std::string"/>
    </Condition>
  </TreeNodesModel>
</root>
